# skill-search — the first step of every task (Cursor)

Self-contained rule: Cursor copies this file into foreign projects, so it uses
absolute URLs only. Source: <https://github.com/ssheleg/make-skill>.

Most installed skills are hidden from the host's skill listing on purpose, so
the listing is not the catalogue. Search the catalogue.

1. Restate the request in one line — the meaning, not the literal phrasing.
2. Decide whether a skill could help. None for a question, an explanation or a
   one-line edit: say so and proceed.
3. Turn the meaning into 3–8 concepts in English AND Russian, plus synonyms and
   the artifact involved (`pdf, документ, extract text, извлечь текст`).
4. Search: `npx --yes sshlg-skills toolkit --find "<concept>, <concept>, …"` —
   it prints id, score, matched concepts, visibility and the SKILL.md path.
   Hidden matches count exactly as listed ones.
5. No node/network, or the launcher does not know `--find` (older than 1.55.0):
   `find -L ~/.agents/skills ~/.claude/skills ~/.cursor/skills -name SKILL.md -exec grep -ilE "^description:.*(<concept>|<concept>)" {} + 2>/dev/null`
   — once; nothing found means no skill, never a loop.
6. Open the SKILL.md of what fits, read it, follow it, and say in one line which
   skill you took (or that none applied).

Building, auditing or publishing a skill is the `make-skill` rule, not this one.
A third-party skill nobody reviewed gets an install review before its scripts run.
